i know you were instrumental in bringing  about a ceasefire in georgia  how badly does this country need a  ceasefire right now  but it's one of these moments when you  know idealism meets realism  i just don't see that ceasefire coming  and the reasons are very simple i think  the stakes for vladimir putin are too  high in georgia it was fairly easy to do  that in five days  but i think in ukraine he's looking for  a big war and a big piece and to be  honest if i was president zelensky there  would be very little that you can  compromise on at the moment  do you think um  actually the world has already caused so  much harm to president putin he feels  like he's got nothing to lose is that  why he's after a big war as you put it  well i think you know he has to stay  today and that is historical great  russia which for him  includes belarus and and ukraine so  that's his sort of vision he wants to  create his own legacy you know he wants  to be there with the stalins and the  ivan the terribles and peter the great  so of russia as a great leader and i  don't think he will do anything that  stands in his way to stop it and the  problem of course is that he can't lose  face and he knows that so therefore you  know we're into the fourth week in this  war and he's not looking extremely good  and probably getting increasingly  desperate as well  so so what are his options now what you  know what will his end game look like  it's very difficult to say i mean i  think there are three options in this  conflict as there quite often are one is  the withdrawal of russian troops  um obviously he's not looking for that  the other one is to split ukraine into  two eastern and west  ukraine and the ukrainians will never  allow that to happen and thirdly is that  he goes all the way and tries to take  over ukraine so i think all of the  options i mean the first one is is the  one that i'd love to see but the two  other ones are are quite horrific to be  honest uh but you know as i said earlier  you know i think this is going to be a  protracted long-term conflict so we're  not talking only about weeks but most  probably months  well it is already horrific as you know  so how do you hold on to a nation  when they begin to hate you  uh for putin  you know obviously you have a couple of  things there you have to remember that  in the dna of of russian culture and  history is this sentiment that the rest  of the world is against russia and  russia is isolated and surrounded you  know attacked from the east by the  mongols in the olden days and now by  served formerly by the nazis and not now  by the westerners so there is this sort  of psyche that you know what's going on  as we we have to fight for for russia  and russian identity but of course it's  going to be a combination of things so  one is the sanctions  you know country that is moving towards  a potential default uh the other one is  the fact that ukraine might be able to  push push back but in russia what you  need is basically a bottom-up uh  movement to get rid of any leader that  that you have and i i think this has to  come from the inside we can't force  change  well what about that idea of pushing  back then how do you see the ukrainians  fighting this and how do you reckon  they've gone so far and how does it  compare with what finland did back in  1939  yeah i mean there are some elements  obviously of the winter war 1939 lasted  for  101 days and there's this sort of  sentiment  almost of heroic nostalgia when we're  watching the ukrainians fighting and the  parallels are there and are not the  parallels that are is that when you're  attacked by a big aggressor for us it  was a soviet union for ukraine it's  russia your sort of sentiment of of  defending and protecting your country  goes beyond the call of duty so to speak  whereas the attacker in other words the  russian soldiers some of them are  wondering you know what are we actually  doing here why are we doing this  the second one is that they are now  being fed  with arms  with financial aid with humanitarian aid  and the longer this goes on the more  convinced i am  that actually uh ukraine and president  zielinski  can push back uh and in that sense  things are not looking very good for  putin this is only an educated guess i'm  not a military expert  well they can push back are you actually  suggesting you think the ukrainians can  win this  well again this is where idealism meets  realism and i can't give you an answer  on that but i would argue that if you're  looking at victory over the hearts minds  and souls of the rest of the world the  ukrainians have already done that and if  you're looking at defeat in terms of  showing the weakness of your military  then putin has already done that  so in in that sense i would argue that  ukrainians have already won but then the  final issue here is the end state you  know is  are they going to be able to  maintain the independence and  sovereignty of ukraine  and its territory that's that's going to  be the key  we were just seeing a piece there from  ireland  questioning their own neutrality you  know where does finland stand on this on  nato neutrality and all those questions  that have been prompted by this war  well i i certainly wouldn't compare  finnish and irish neutrality because  ours  during the cold war was one of necessity  with 1 340 kilometers of border  with russia  when the cold war ended we immediately  ditched the notion of neutrality joined  the european union joined the euro  a very close partnership with nato  participate in nato operations and also  in nato training our military is more  nato compatible than that of many member  states and we were very quick to send  arms to ukraine as well  but this has obviously shifted public  opinion on nato membership you know we  used to be 50 against 24. now the latest  opinion post 62 in favor of nato  membership and 16 against so if i may  predict with my academic glasses on the  train has left the station and  eventually finland will join nato  very interesting um so many implications  from this conflict certainly professor  alexander stubb former prime minister of  finland thank you very much indeed for  your time much appreciated  my pleasure
 

